==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|stiffenes}}, {{m|enm|styffenesse}}, {{m|enm|styfnesse}}; equivalent to {{af|en|stiff|-ness}}. Perhaps merging with {{cog|enm|stithnesse}}, {{m|enm|stithnysse}}, from {{der|en|ang|stīþness|t=stiffness}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-stiffness.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{U|rigidity}} or a measure of rigidity.
# {{U|inflexibility}} or a [[measure]] of inflexibility.
# {{U|inelegance}}; a lack of [[relaxedness]].
#: {{ux|en|His '''stiffness''' hampered the conversation.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1699|author={{w|Sir William Temple, 1st Baronet|William Temple}}|title=Heads designed for an essay on conversations|url=http://essays.quotidiana.org/temple/heads_for_conversation/
|passage=Study gives strength to the mind; conversation, grace: the first apt to give '''stiffness''', the other suppleness: one gives substance and form to the statue, the other polishes it.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Charudutta Panigrahi|title=The Intimacy Of Slangs|magazine=w:Odisha News|url=https://odishabytes.com/the-intimacy-of-slangs/|date=2021-09-03|passage=After years when I met a friend inside the aircraft and could sense '''stiffness''' in the conversation, a whiff of mild slang was the ice breaker, followed by loads of campus nostalgia.}}
# {{U|muscular}} [[tension]] due to unaccustomed or excessive exercise or work; [[soreness]].

